= Meetings with the Opposition

The sun was beginning to rise over the horizon as seen from the eastern windows of the presidential palace. Ecevit peered down the window and saw the black limousines carrying the leaders of the opposition stop by the entrance one by one. He had called them up to brief them on the exact developments in Cyprus and express the attitude of the government. Him calling them to the presidential palace was a signal too in regards to the government's stance, intra-party meetings were held in the presence of the president only in cases of national emergency and foreign interventions. He hoped the leaders would catch the hint and realize the gravity of the situation. While wandering through his thoughts, he heard the door click and open.

<span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #750a6f; font-weight: bold;";>Demirel</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/demirell.jpg">Süleyman Demirel. The chairman of AP and the leader of the main opposition. He is known for his political maneuvering and survival instincts.</span></span>, span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">, <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #4a4d04; font-weight: bold ";>Bozbeyli</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/bozbeyli.jpeg">Ferruh Bozbeyli. The chairman of the right wing DP. He is a young lawyer who defended the former members of the Democrat Party during the political trails that followed the 1960 coup.</span></span>, <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #5a5f63; font-weight: bold;";>Feyzioğlu</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/feyzioglu.jpg">Turhan Feyzioğlu. The chairman of CGP, he was expected to be İnönü's successor before he was overshadowed by Ecevit's and the Left of Center's rise.</span></span> and <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #7c8287; font-weight: bold;";>Nihat Erim</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/demirell.jpg">Nihat Erim. A prominent Right Kemalist, he was the prime minister in the cabinet that followed the 12 March Memorandum in 1971.</span></span> stepped into the room one by one and took their seats. Admist curious eyes, Ecevit signalled national defense minister [+ reichswehr_minister +] to begin the briefing.

'There is a general consensus among the international community that the coup against <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="color: #1269C7;>Makarios</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/makarios.jpeg">Archbishop Makarios III. The spiritual leader of the Greek Cypriots and the president of the Republic of Cyprus since its foundation in 1960.</span></span> has been orchastrated from a foreign country. We couldn't get the US's response as quickly as we desired. The ambassador couldn't be found in the consolate. We contacted the acting chief of mission. He only expressed his private opinion and underlined how <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="color: #1269C7;>Makarios</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/makarios.jpeg">Archbishop Makarios III. The spiritual leader of the Greek Cypriots and the president of the Republic of Cyprus since its foundation in 1960.</span></span> was supposedly under Communist influence and thus said it may be decent news if the Greek Junta took action in order to put an end to this cozying. However he also reported that he thought the coup may be a move for span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="color: #011E56;>ENOSIS</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/makarios.jpeg">Enosis is a Greek irredentist ideology that advocates for the unification of Greece and Cyprus, justified through the ethnic composition of the island. While endorsed by much of the Greek political spectrum, its most ardent supporters are the nationalists.</span></span> which would be worrying news for regional stability. We also got news from span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Dr. Kissinger<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/kissinger.jpeg">Henry Kissinger. The US Secretary of State. He is known for his fervent Anti-Communism, devotion to 'Realpolitik' and as, well, the butcher of Indochina.</span></span> who refrained from making any comments except for arguing against a new conflict. We are also in talks with the Soviet ambassy. Yugoslavia, Italy and Canada are worried greatly. The messages from the secretaries of the UN and NATO are the same too; they don't want a war to arise between the two countries.'

Seeing as this wasn't much more than a reiteration of the morning news, Ecevit set out to explain the attitude of the government. 

'The Greek reinforcements for their garrison on the island have been sent to the island a day before the normal date. We have called them and requested that the reinforcements be stopped. We are thinking of activating the terms of the guarantorship treaty. However due to their involvement in the coup, we don't consider Greece a guarantor anymore and instead have reached out to the United Kingdom. I will be going to London in order to negotiate a common course of action.

<span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #4a4d04; font-weight: bold ";>Ferruh Bozbeyli</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/bozbeyli.jpeg">Ferruh Bozbeyli. The chairman of the right wing DP. He is a young lawyer who defended the former members of the Democrat Party during the political trails that followed the 1960 coup.</span></span> spoke first. 'This event isn't a conflict among the Greeks as some argue. There is apperant involvement by Greece. I think the situation is very critical. The <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">London-Zurich Agreements</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/london.jpeg">The London-Zurich agreements of 1959 laid down the foundation of the Republic of Cyprus as a unitary state with Turkey, Greece and the United Kingdom acting as guarantors of the island's security. The countries have a right to intervene in case 'the state of affairs' in Cyprus break down.</span></span> are completely annulled at this point.'

<span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #750a6f; font-weight: bold;";>Demirel</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/demirell.jpg">Süleyman Demirel. The chairman of AP and the leader of the main opposition. He is known for his political maneuvering and survival instincts.</span></span>, span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"> was more cautious. 'I wonder what the Greeks truly aim with this intervention. I am worried by the prospect of our garrison and brothers in Cyprus getting massacred. Does the government have a clear plan on what to do in this scenario? There must surely have been considerations that took place before.'

<span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #5a5f63; font-weight: bold;";>Feyzioğlu</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/feyzioglu.jpg">Turhan Feyzioğlu. The chairman of CGP, he was expected to be İnönü's successor before he was overshadowed by Ecevit's and the Left of Center's rise.</span></span> was more reluctant too and asked the government to prioritize the diplomatic means. 'We can take action to exclude the Cypriot government from the European Council. We can impose pressure to prevent Sampson's junta from getting international recognition. We can convince the other countries of our position through a diplomatic offensive. We can use NATO for this purpose.'

<span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="style:"color: #7c8287; font-weight: bold;";>Nihat Erim</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/demirell.jpg">Nihat Erim. A prominent Right Kemalist, he was the prime minister in the cabinet that followed the 12 March Memorandum in 1971.</span></span> called for caution too. 'The US may be the one behind this coup. Even if it's not clear yet, I suspect Washington may be viewing the Sampson Junta with sympathy. The government has to figure out their allegiances of the US and USSR before acting.

As Ecevit prepared to answer each one of these reactions, he wondered how almost none of them could realize the gravity of the situation.

- @root: And he thought of his next stop: London.







